+## Task 2: arbitrary nonnested schedules refute a uniform X/N claim
+
+PROVED nonnested counterexample theorem R12-U. There is a sequence A_X in [1,X], with |A_X|/X->1/4 and macroscopic profile p(t)=(1-t)/2, that is locally 2-periodic on cells of length L comparable to sqrt(X), but has M(A_X)=O(X^(3/4)*sqrt(log X))=o(X). Hence neither M>=c(d,p0)*X/N nor M>=c(d,p0)*X*sqrt(p0/N), with a positive constant depending only on d,p0, is true for ALL locally periodic nonnested schedules, even at N=2, p0=1/2, and limiting d=1/4. This is no counterexample to square-root Chowla: the displayed bound is much larger than sqrt(X).
+
+PROVED construction and proof. Take X=T*L with T,L tending to infinity and L comparable to T. On cell j of L consecutive integers choose independently its entire contents with probability p_j=(1-(j-1/2)/T)/2, or choose none. Force the first cell instead to contain only its even integers; this costs at most L in the polynomial. Each other cell has E_t=Z_2 or the empty set, so the model is 2-periodic inside cells, and is not nested. The expected coefficient array differs in l1 by O(L) from the sampled triangular PD profile (1-k/(X+1))/2, whose cosine sum is >=-1/4. For the centered block error, each independent summand has magnitude at most L at every angle. Hoeffding on an O(X^3)-point circle grid, plus the deterministic derivative bound O(X^2), gives with probability tending to one
+
+    sup_x |block_error(x)| <= C*sqrt(X*L*log X).
+
+PROVED the cell count also concentrates and its weak macroscopic measures converge to p(t)dt, using a countable-test diagonal if desired. Thus a realization exists with the claimed density, profile, and global minimum bound. The first cell has exact local residue density 1/2; the continuous limiting profile also has p(0)=1/2. Since L/X->0, N<<L<<X holds. A global low-complexity assumption on the number of time changes is therefore indispensable if an X/N statement is meant outside the nested class.
+
+NUMERICAL supporting VM probe, seed 2026101913: X=65536, 262144, 1048576, with L=256,512,1024 and N=2, gave sampled M/X=0.0729651, 0.0360164, 0.0342205. These are uncertified global estimates; the theorem above supplies the asymptotic all-circle upper bound. Independent 220-bit Arb evaluations certify the saved negative points, not an upper bound on their actual M. Exact run endpoints and grid indices are saved in `nonnested_X*.json`.
